// Three all-boolean objectives: one R constraint and two P (G F) constraints.
// With probability p: stay at s=1 forever (R1=1, G F s=1 satisfied, G F s=2 not).
// With probability 1-p: immediately leave to s=2 (R1=3, G F s=2 satisfied, G F s=1 not).
// P[G F s=1]=p, P[G F s=2]=1-p, E[R1]=3-2p.

// RESULT: true
// Need 3-2p>=1 (R1), p>=0.4 (G F s=1), 1-p>=0.4 (G F s=2): p in [0.4,0.6], R1 in [1.8,2.2]. Feasible.
multi(R{"R1"}>=1.0 [ C ], P>=0.4 [ G F s=1 ], P>=0.4 [ G F s=2 ])

// RESULT: false
// R1 = 3-2p <= 2.2 in feasible range [0.4,0.6]; 3.1 is unachievable.
multi(R{"R1"}>=3.1 [ C ], P>=0.4 [ G F s=1 ], P>=0.4 [ G F s=2 ])

// RESULT: true
// R1>=2.0: 3-2p>=2 => p<=0.5; combined with p in [0.4,0.6] gives p in [0.4,0.5]. Feasible.
multi(R{"R1"}>=2.0 [ C ], P>=0.4 [ G F s=1 ], P>=0.4 [ G F s=2 ])
